    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-213421",
      "sequence_position": 1,
      "problem": "Consider the following relation over the sets $A=\\{1,2,3,4,5\\}$ and $B=\\{2,4,6,8\\}.$ \n\n$$\nR=\\{(1,6),(2,2),(2,4),(2,6),(3,6)\\}\n$$\n\nWhat are the domain and range of this relation?\n\nThe domain is {{field-1}}\n\nThe range is {{field-2}}",
      "worked_solution": "Given a relation $R⊆A\\times B,$ \n\n- the *domain* of $R$ is the set containing all elements from $A$ that appear as the first component of the ordered pairs in $R,$ and\n\n- the *range* of $R$ is the set containing all elements from $B$ that appear as the second component of the ordered pairs in $R.$\n\nWith that in mind, we examine the given relation.\n\n- Let's consider the first components of the pairs belonging to our relation:\n\n$$\nR=\\{(1,6),(2,2),(2,4),(2,6),(3,6)\\}\n$$\n\nThe set of first components is $\\{1,2,2,2,3\\}=\\{1,2,3\\}.$ \nHence, the domain is $\\{1,2,3\\}.$\n\n- Let's consider the second components of the pairs belonging to our relation:\n\n$$\nR=\\{(1,6),(2,2),(2,4),(2,6),(3,6)\\}\n$$\n\nThe set of second components is $\\{6,2,4,6,6\\}=\\{2,4,6\\}.$ \nHence, the range is $\\{2,4,6\\}.$",
